[00:30.00] We lost the train to the plate on a harmonica and a char by Woody Guthrie from Okinawa, Oklahoma.
[00:56.00] Nobody knows what that lost train means because he's ridden off the red ball plate from one end of the country to another.
[01:04.00] In a few minutes we're going to begin our conversation with Woody Guthrie about life in the Southwest, about where he's from and where he went from one end.
[01:20.00] Woody Guthrie is I guess about 30 years old from the looks of him, but he's seen more in those 30 years than most men see before they sat on him.
[01:34.00] He hasn't sat on a warm house or a warm office and seen what he's been interested in looking at and done out in the world.
[01:44.00] He's looked at the faces of 100 men and women, he's lived in a hobo jungle.
[01:49.00] He's performed on ticket lines, he's sung his way through areas with bars and bars, he's played in Oklahoma, Oklahoma and listened to that red ball roll.
[02:20.00] How long was it to go that you were born in Okinawa?
[02:26.00] 28 years, you put in there a guest that I was born there in July the 14th, 1912.
[02:33.00] The year that the president, Rudro Wilson, was nominated.
[02:39.00] What did your family do? What kind of people were they? Where'd they come from?
[02:46.00] Well they come in there from Texas in the early day, my dad got to Oklahoma right after statehood.
[02:53.00] He was the first clerk of the county court in Okinawa, Oklahoma, after statehood.
[03:02.00] He's known as one of them old hard-hitting, fist-fighting Democrats, you know, that run for office down there.
[03:11.00] And they used to miscount the votes all the time, so every time my dad went to town,
[03:17.00] it was common, the first question I'd ask him when he'd come riding in on a horse that evening,
[03:22.00] I'd say, well, how many fights do you have today?
[03:26.00] And then he'd take me up on his knee and he'd proceed to tell me who he is fighting and why and all about it.
[03:36.00] Where did you live on a farm?
[03:39.00] Well, no, I was born there in that little town.
[03:44.00] My dad built a six-room house, cost him about $7,000 or $8,000,
[03:50.00] and the day after he got the house built, it burned down.
[03:53.00] What kind of a place was Okinawa? How big was it?
[03:57.00] Well, you remember it when you were a kid.
[03:59.00] Well, in them days, his little town about $1,500 and then $2,000.
[04:03.00] A few years later, it got up to about $5,000.
[04:06.00] Struck some pretty rich oil pools all around there,
[04:09.00] Garrison City and Slick City and Cromwell and Seminole and Bowlegs and Sandsprings and Spring Hill.
[04:16.00] All up and down the whole country there, they got oil, got some pretty nice oil fields around Okinawa.
[04:22.00] Did any of the oil come in your family?
[04:25.00] No, we got the grease.
[04:28.00] I'm getting oil.
[04:30.00] Well, what kind of people lived in Okinawa?
[04:33.00] Oh, just sort of a farming class of people, a town didn't amount to much except on a Saturday,
[04:40.00] you know, when all the farming people had come in there to have a trades day
[04:45.00] or give away a new buggy in a box at the back there
[04:49.00] or buy them a new pair of buttoned shoes or some sort of stuff like that.
[04:56.00] There's always a big Saturday.
[04:58.00] All the farmers that all come to town had a big time to celebrate,
[05:03.00] but the population of people that live there right now is about one-fourth Indians
[05:09.00] and one-fourth Negroes and one-half white.
[05:14.00] Do all those people mix on pretty equal terms or not? How does it go?
[05:19.00] Well, no, not what I'd call equal terms.
[05:25.00] There's been a lot of shooting scrapes and fights.
[05:30.00] But anyway, there are some crazy way they've got to looking at the colored situation
[05:43.00] ever since I was a kid growing up, I've always found time to start and talk to these colored people
[05:51.00] because I found them to be full of jokes, what I mean, and wisdom.
[06:00.00] I learned how to play a French harp off a boy that shines shoes down there.
[06:04.00] I was passing the barbershop one day and I was about fifteen or sixteen years old
[06:09.00] and there was a big barefooted boy up laying in there
[06:12.00] and had his feet turned up towards me and he was playing the harmonica.
